[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: laptop0
diagnosis_text:
The reported “gateway down” on router0’s VLAN 20 is not observed. On router0, the VLAN-20 sub-interface eth0.20 is present and operational: ip addr shows eth0.20 <UP,LOWER_UP> with 172.16.20.1/24, and the kernel route includes 172.16.20.0/24 dev eth0.20 src 172.16.20.1. Interface counters show RX/TX activity, further indicating the interface is up.

The actual affected component is the host-side default gateway configuration on laptop0. Laptop0’s routing table has default via 172.16.20.99 dev eth0, not via the correct gateway 172.16.20.1. Mechanism: For any off-subnet destination, laptop0 ARPs for 172.16.20.99 on VLAN 20; because that next-hop does not exist, ARP fails and traffic never leaves the local segment. Intra-VLAN communication still works because those destinations are on 172.16.20.0/24 and are reached directly via ARP, matching the symptom that only off-VLAN reachability is broken.

Localization: misconfigured default route on laptop0’s eth0; router0’s eth0.20 is healthy. There is no down VLAN-20 interface on router0.

Root cause: Laptop0 is configured with an incorrect default gateway (172.16.20.99 instead of 172.16.20.1).

Corrective fix: Update laptop0’s default route to use 172.16.20.1 (router0’s VLAN-20 SVI), or correct its DHCP/static IP settings so the proper gateway is set. No action is required on router0; its VLAN-20 sub-interface is up.
=== END ===